<?php
/**
 * Spartrak — the added-to-wish-list toast, as the THREE parts Figma's alert
 * draws.
 *
 * FIGMA SSOT: 1286:29513 ("Alert - Dark", success) — a title, a supporting
 * line and an action. Identical shell and identical structure to the
 * add-to-cart toast, which is the point: two saves, one component.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT HAD TO GO
 * ===========================================================================
 * The stock template is one sentence with a link buried in it:
 *
 *     %1 has been added to your Wish List.
 *     Click <a href="%2">here</a> to continue shopping.
 *
 * Three separate problems, all visible in the reported screenshot:
 *
 *   1. IT WAS THREE LINES OF 16/600 TYPE. A product name plus two clauses at
 *      the title weight, wrapping, is a toast the size of a dialog.
 *   2. "CLICK HERE TO CONTINUE SHOPPING" NOW DESCRIBES NOTHING. The heart is
 *      an AJAX toggle (Spartrak\Wishlist\Controller\Ajax\Toggle) — the shopper
 *      never went anywhere, so there is nothing to continue FROM. The one link
 *      that is genuinely useful is the wish list itself, and it belongs in the
 *      action slot rather than mid-sentence.
 *   3. IT READ BADLY IN ARABIC. An inline anchor inside a bidirectional
 *      sentence puts the link and the trailing full stop on whichever side the
 *      bidi algorithm resolves them to — which is why the screenshot shows an
 *      underlined "here" alone on its own line with the stop ahead of the text.
 *      Splitting the parts means no run of text has to be bidirectional.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * THE MARKUP IS CONSTRAINED BY MAGENTO'S OWN JS ESCAPER
 * ===========================================================================
 * On the customer-data path this HTML goes through
 * Magento_Theme/js/view/messages::prepareMessageForHtml -> Magento_Security/
 * js/escaper, whose allowed tags are div/span/b/strong/i/em/u/a and whose
 * generally-allowed attributes include `class` and `href`.
 *
 * So: spans and one anchor, carrying classes and an href, and nothing else.
 * A <p>, a <button> or any data-* attribute would survive the server-rendered
 * path and be silently stripped on the AJAX one — and since this toast is now
 * raised almost exclusively over AJAX, that is the path that matters.
 *
 * Same constraint, same shape and the same three class names as
 * Magento_Checkout/templates/messages/addCartSuccessMessage.phtml; see that
 * file for the full reasoning on why overriding a complex-message template is
 * the platform's own extension point for this.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * ESCAPING
 * ===========================================================================
 * The product name is merchant data and goes through escapeHtml; the wish list
 * URL through escapeUrl. The surrounding markup is a literal in this file.
 *
 * @var \Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template $block
 * @var \Magento\Framework\Escaper $escaper
 */

$productName = (string) $block->getData('product_name');

/*
 * `referer` is what core's own Add controller calls this slot, so the data key
 * is unchanged and BOTH callers work: Spartrak's AJAX toggle passes the wish
 * list URL, and core's controller — still reachable, e.g. from the PDP's own
 * native heart — passes the page the shopper came from. Either is a sensible
 * destination for "view your wish list"; the AJAX one is the accurate one.
 */
$wishlistUrl = (string) $block->getData('referer');
?>
<span class="spartrak-toast__text"><span class="spartrak-toast__title"><?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('%1 saved to your wish list', $productName)) ?></span><span class="spartrak-toast__body"><?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('It is waiting for you whenever you are ready.')) ?></span></span><?php if ($wishlistUrl !== '') : ?><a class="spartrak-toast__action" href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($wishlistUrl) ?>"><?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('View wish list')) ?></a><?php endif; ?>
